[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-51054"] :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+x, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(-1)=(-1)^{2}+1=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}({x}^{3}+x) \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+(-1) \\\\  & =-2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-71068/selection/af5580a233ee3709064249d6732fc822dfb93f38caf90c025deb51b127e90b2c" :answer/id #uuid "8e63c9f9-177f-5b35-8632-17b0012d74b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71068/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "8450d8cf-2a55-5c0b-b6ca-41a99c6809a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71068/selection/4ef0fcc340e40ad6bcef1c832215cd28abf240aabeed43296dfece28d20d1935" :answer/id #uuid "7b9e4522-c31d-5a7f-bac7-b6645a714993" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71068/selection/05402db46107d70ece210f0bd5e24b332ae4f43bd474d592035c3e3c92625629" :answer/id #uuid "676972b0-9c38-555e-aa6c-98b2576391e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71068/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "71a6bb71-4604-51f2-8c37-2d5b05a0a08e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71068/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6edc00b-3d38-589c-898c-269b98486106" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-71068/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-71068/selection/af5580a233ee3709064249d6732fc822dfb93f38caf90c025deb51b127e90b2c" "q-71068/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-71068/selection/4ef0fcc340e40ad6bcef1c832215cd28abf240aabeed43296dfece28d20d1935" "q-71068/selection/05402db46107d70ece210f0bd5e24b332ae4f43bd474d592035c3e3c92625629" "q-71068/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-71068" :question/id #uuid "22f74da2-9345-55bb-831c-5f5f24cacef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-71068" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)=8,$ then what is the value of $\\sqrt{f(-1)+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(-1)={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)=8.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{f(-1)+1} & =\\sqrt{8+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-71068/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71068"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-71071/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "5af31e22-830a-53c3-8f12-4cacbb72a828" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71071/selection/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea" :answer/id #uuid "d58972ba-c40c-53b6-bc5e-7b0121468430" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71071/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "92725a5b-0cb3-5c0a-b256-4d7c8498acb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71071/selection/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" :answer/id #uuid "a4ea99a2-5320-550c-b408-59fb4d23af63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71071/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "d55ffa54-16ff-5309-9227-f9eddcec0dd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71071/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6ac9bc9e-08a9-59ce-9726-fe81c86b198c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-71071/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer-field/choices ["q-71071/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-71071/selection/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea" "q-71071/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" "q-71071/selection/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" "q-71071/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-71071" :question/id #uuid "eeea373f-95bb-5842-a9f9-990d433fa820" :question/math-academy-id "q-71071" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ and $f(4)=2,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to 4}{({x}^{2}-6f(x))}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}{({x}^{2}-6f(x))}^{2} & ={({4}^{2}-6(2))}^{2} \\\\  & ={(16-12)}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-71071/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71071"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-71080/selection/bfafb0345295d7a1f5de847f7fe1a32272dd270e2a859c61d9bd38f210dbce17" :answer/id #uuid "02592502-597f-51a4-ac46-8f134902e392" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71080/selection/fd6426755d22f4317347219d3d99e80396aad25e3c4556d207fd2f29ecf4a12a" :answer/id #uuid "e80ae7c5-8d21-5195-8f84-1afce5f07966" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71080/selection/92861585f929682e1983ae5c728db701972383d1b14a551ffdf0b5ac5f848be0" :answer/id #uuid "8401d42d-29e4-51cb-be08-d69ea97c9132" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(2)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71080/selection/8bb1b1393896e749c376b11abd237d96fb660d550bd060e1f0467570ae6af2d9" :answer/id #uuid "a211603e-ede9-52c2-a8e1-d000c64df542" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71080/selection/cf93754e682e9ae90e30f7712faafc2f53dcf10208fb2ff2c79e1c9a7c3de976" :answer/id #uuid "dc732819-aa43-5652-923d-8022d0e979a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71080/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f0bde795-7c9d-5fcf-adaa-aa6a537785b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-71080/selection/8bb1b1393896e749c376b11abd237d96fb660d550bd060e1f0467570ae6af2d9" :answer-field/choices ["q-71080/selection/bfafb0345295d7a1f5de847f7fe1a32272dd270e2a859c61d9bd38f210dbce17" "q-71080/selection/fd6426755d22f4317347219d3d99e80396aad25e3c4556d207fd2f29ecf4a12a" "q-71080/selection/92861585f929682e1983ae5c728db701972383d1b14a551ffdf0b5ac5f848be0" "q-71080/selection/8bb1b1393896e749c376b11abd237d96fb660d550bd060e1f0467570ae6af2d9" "q-71080/selection/cf93754e682e9ae90e30f7712faafc2f53dcf10208fb2ff2c79e1c9a7c3de976"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-71080" :question/id #uuid "a5fc9af3-149c-5207-9936-917f743c751e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71080" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-2x+3, & x<2 \\\\ 2x-3, & x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(2)=2(2)-3=1,$ so $f(2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-2x+3) \\\\  & =-2(2)+3 \\\\  & =-1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(2x-3) \\\\  & =2(2)-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. Therefore, ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist. $\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-71080/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71080"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342242/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "76210bf0-b3ae-5675-aa60-65ed8fba1577" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342242/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "29f42fa8-490c-526d-b6af-3979ac2774c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342242/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer-field/choices ["q-342242/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342242" :question/id #uuid "b0898607-40ba-5160-b291-bbfc5674ac89" :question/math-academy-id "q-342242" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6$ and $f(6)=14,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}\\sqrt{f(x)+{x}^{2}-1}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 6}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 6}f(x)=f(6)=14.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}\\sqrt{f(x)+{x}^{2}-1} & =\\sqrt{14+{6}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342242/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342242"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
